/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/sscp.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("sscp", [["ss1",0,1,"Two canons of the ISC2 Code of Ethics appear to conflict in a situation you face. Which canon takes precedence?",["Provide diligent and competent service to principals","Advance and protect the profession","Protect society, the common good, necessary public trust and confidence, and the infrastructure","Act honorably, honestly, justly, responsibly, and legally"],2,"The canons are applied in order, and protecting society and the infrastructure comes first.","Outline 1.1",["Serving principals is the third canon, so it yields to the duty to protect society when the two conflict.","Advancing the profession is the fourth and lowest-priority canon, so it never overrides the others.",null,"Acting honorably and legally is the second canon; it is important but still ranks below protecting society."]],["ss2",0,1,"A help desk technician whose only job is resetting user passwords has Domain Admin rights. What is the BEST action?",["Grant only the delegated password-reset permission and remove Domain Admin membership","Keep Domain Admin rights but enable detailed auditing of the account","Have the technician sign an acceptable use policy","Rotate the Domain Admin password every month"],0,"Least privilege means giving only the access the job needs; auditing and policies don't remove the excess privilege.","Outline 1.2",[null,"Auditing only records what the over-privileged account does; it leaves the excess Domain Admin rights in place.","An acceptable use policy is a directive control that sets expectations but does not remove the unnecessary privileges.","Rotating the password protects the credential but the technician still holds far more rights than the job requires."]],["ss3",0,1,"Which control BEST prevents a single employee from both creating a new vendor and approving payments to it?",["Job rotation","Mandatory vacation","Need to know","Separation of duties"],3,"Separation of duties splits a sensitive process so one person can't complete it alone; the others mainly help detect fraud after the fact.","Outline 1.2",["Job rotation moves people between roles so fraud may be discovered later; it does not stop one person completing both steps.","Mandatory vacation is a detective control that exposes ongoing fraud while the person is away, not a preventive split of duties.","Need to know limits access to information by necessity but does not require two different people for a sensitive transaction.",null]],["ss4",0,1,"A legacy controller cannot support MFA, so it is placed on an isolated VLAN reachable only through a monitored jump host. What type of control is this?",["Deterrent","Compensating","Directive","Corrective"],1,"A compensating control is an alternative safeguard used when the primary control can't be implemented.","Outline 1.3",["A deterrent control discourages attackers, such as warning signs; isolation here actually replaces a missing control.",null,"A directive control tells people what to do, like a policy or procedure, rather than technically restricting access.","A corrective control fixes damage after an incident, such as restoring from backup, which is not what the VLAN does."]],["ss5",0,1,"Which statement BEST describes defense in depth?",["Deploying the strongest available firewall at the network perimeter","Layering multiple independent controls so the failure of one does not expose the asset","Concentrating security spending on the most valuable asset","Encrypting all data so other controls become unnecessary"],1,"Defense in depth relies on several overlapping layers rather than any single strong control.","Outline 1.2",["A single strong perimeter firewall is one layer; if it fails or is bypassed nothing else protects the asset.",null,"Focusing spending on one asset is a prioritization decision, not the layering of multiple independent controls.","Encryption is one control; relying on it alone is the opposite of defense in depth."]],["ss6",0,1,"An attacker alters payroll amounts while the file is in transit to the bank. Which security principle has been violated?",["Confidentiality","Availability","Privacy","Integrity"],3,"Unauthorized modification of data is a loss of integrity.","Outline 1.2",["Confidentiality is about unauthorized disclosure; here the data was changed, not just read.","Availability concerns timely access to data and systems, which was not lost in this scenario.","Privacy concerns the handling of personal data about individuals, while the attack here was unauthorized modification.",null]],["ss7",0,1,"Why should implemented security controls be tested and reviewed periodically?",["To confirm they still operate as intended as systems, threats and business needs change","Because auditors require every control to be replaced annually","To reduce the number of controls that must be documented","To shift accountability for controls from management to IT"],0,"Controls drift and environments change, so ongoing verification shows they remain effective.","Outline 1.4",[null,"Auditors expect controls to be tested for effectiveness, not replaced every year.","Testing controls does not reduce documentation; results usually add to the documented evidence.","Management remains accountable for controls; reviews verify effectiveness rather than shifting accountability."]],["ss8",0,1,"What is the MOST important prerequisite for applying appropriate protection to information assets?",["A signed contract with a managed security provider","Full-disk encryption on every endpoint","An accurate inventory with an assigned owner and classification for each asset","A completed penetration test of the network"],2,"You can't protect what you don't know you have; ownership and classification drive which controls are applied.","Outline 1.5",["A managed security provider can help operate controls, but it cannot protect assets that have not been identified and classified.","Full-disk encryption is one control; deciding where and how strongly to protect data first requires knowing the assets and their value.",null,"A penetration test finds exploitable weaknesses but assumes you already know what assets are in scope and how valuable they are."]],["ss9",0,1,"Who is ultimately responsible for deciding the classification level of a customer database?",["The data custodian","The database administrator","The security administrator","The data owner"],3,"The data owner, a business role, is accountable for classification; custodians implement the resulting controls.","Outline 1.5",["The custodian implements and maintains protections such as backups; they do not decide the classification level.","The DBA is a technical custodian role that applies controls, not the business role accountable for classifying the data.","The security administrator configures controls according to the classification but does not set it.",null]],["ss10",0,1,"Solid-state drives holding confidential data are being retired and will leave the organization. Which method is MOST appropriate?",["Degaussing the drives","Physical destruction such as shredding","Performing a quick format","Deleting all partitions"],1,"Degaussing does not work on flash storage and formatting leaves recoverable data; destruction ensures the data can't be recovered.","Outline 1.5",["Degaussing relies on magnetic fields and does not erase flash memory, so data on SSDs may remain.",null,"A quick format only rewrites file system structures, leaving the underlying data easily recoverable.","Deleting partitions removes the partition table entries but leaves the data blocks intact and recoverable."]],["ss11",0,1,"A technician wants to push an urgent firewall rule change to production during business hours. What should the technician do FIRST?",["Apply the change now and document it afterwards","Apply the change and monitor for complaints","Submit it through the change management process, using the emergency change procedure if justified","Ask the firewall vendor to approve the rule"],2,"Even urgent changes need review and approval, with emergency procedures that still record impact and backout steps.","Outline 1.6",["Making the change first bypasses review and approval, which is exactly what change management is meant to prevent.","Monitoring for complaints is not a substitute for impact analysis, approval and a backout plan.",null,"The vendor has no authority over your organization's changes; approval must come through your own change process."]],["ss12",0,1,"Which metric BEST shows that phishing awareness training is changing behaviour?",["Falling click rates and rising report rates across repeated phishing simulations","The percentage of staff who completed the training module","The number of training sessions delivered this year","Average scores on the end-of-course quiz"],0,"Completion and quiz scores measure attendance and recall, not whether people act differently.","Outline 1.7",[null,"Completion percentage shows who finished the module, not whether they now spot and report phishing.","Counting sessions delivered measures training effort, not any change in how staff behave.","Quiz scores measure short-term recall of the material, not real-world behaviour when a phishing email arrives."]],["ss13",0,1,"Employees are holding the door open for people following them into a secure area. Which control BEST addresses this?",["An access control vestibule (mantrap) that admits one person per authentication","Additional CCTV cameras at the entrance","A warning sign about tailgating","A visitor sign-in log at reception"],0,"A vestibule physically prevents tailgating; cameras and signs only detect or deter it.","Outline 1.8",[null,"CCTV is a detective control that records tailgating but does not physically stop it.","A warning sign is a deterrent that people can ignore; it does not enforce one person per entry.","A visitor log records who was admitted but does nothing to stop employees letting others follow them in."]],["ss14",0,2,"Which combination provides true multifactor authentication?",["A password and the answer to a security question","A fingerprint scan and a palm scan","A password and a one-time code from a hardware token","A PIN and a passphrase"],2,"MFA requires different factor types; the other options use two items from the same factor.","Outline 2.1",["A password and a security question answer are both something you know, so this is single-factor authentication.","Fingerprint and palm scans are both biometrics (something you are), so only one factor type is used.",null,"A PIN and a passphrase are both knowledge factors, so combining them does not create MFA."]],["ss15",0,2,"For a biometric system protecting a data center, which error rate should be minimized FIRST?",["False rejection rate","False acceptance rate","Failure-to-enroll rate","Throughput rate"],1,"False acceptances let unauthorized people in, which is the greater risk for a high-security area.","Outline 2.1",["False rejections inconvenience legitimate users but do not let unauthorized people into the data center.",null,"Failure-to-enroll affects how many users can register with the system, not whether impostors are admitted.","Throughput rate measures how fast people are processed, a usability metric rather than a security error rate."]],["ss16",0,2,"What is the GREATEST security risk introduced by single sign-on?",["Users must remember more passwords","Authentication traffic increases across the network","Help desk password reset calls increase","Compromise of one credential can grant access to many systems"],3,"SSO concentrates access behind one credential, so protecting it with MFA becomes critical.","Outline 2.1",["SSO reduces the number of passwords users must remember; that is one of its benefits.","SSO typically reduces repeated authentication traffic, and network load is not its main security risk.","SSO usually reduces password reset calls because users manage fewer credentials.",null]],["ss17",0,2,"Which standard exchanges XML-based authentication and authorization assertions between an identity provider and a service provider for browser SSO?",["LDAP","RADIUS","SAML","Kerberos"],2,"SAML defines assertions passed between an IdP and SP in federated web SSO.","Outline 2.2",["LDAP is a directory access protocol used to query and authenticate against a directory, not to pass federated XML assertions.","RADIUS provides AAA for network access such as VPN and Wi-Fi, not browser-based federated SSO.",null,"Kerberos uses tickets within a realm or domain and is not an XML assertion format for web SSO between organizations."]],["ss18",0,2,"A web application needs to verify a user's identity using the user's existing social account. Which protocol adds an authentication layer on top of OAuth 2.0 for this purpose?",["OpenID Connect","OAuth 2.0 on its own","TACACS+","NTLM"],0,"OAuth 2.0 handles delegated authorization; OpenID Connect adds ID tokens to authenticate the user.","Outline 2.2",[null,"OAuth 2.0 by itself only delegates authorization to resources; it does not define a standard way to authenticate the user.","TACACS+ is an AAA protocol for administering network devices, not for web sign-in with social accounts.","NTLM is a legacy Windows challenge-response protocol and is unrelated to OAuth-based social logins."]],["ss19",0,2,"Domain A trusts domain B, and domain B trusts domain C, with all trusts transitive. What is the MAIN security concern for domain A?",["Domain A users lose access to domain B","Kerberos tickets can no longer be issued in domain A","Domain C administrators must reset domain A passwords","Users in domain C may reach domain A resources without domain A approving a trust with C"],3,"Transitive trust extends access implicitly, so organizations should review and limit trust relationships.","Outline 2.2",["Trust relationships extend access; they do not remove domain A users' access to domain B.","Establishing trusts does not stop domain A's own controllers from issuing Kerberos tickets.","Trusts do not give domain C administrators a duty to reset passwords in domain A.",null]],["ss20",0,2,"An employee moves from accounts payable to marketing. What is the MOST important identity management action?",["Add marketing permissions to the existing account","Remove the accounts payable access no longer needed while provisioning the marketing role","Create a second account for the marketing role","Wait for the next quarterly access review to adjust permissions"],1,"Removing old access on transfer prevents privilege creep and preserves separation of duties.","Outline 2.3",["Adding new permissions without removing the old ones is exactly how privilege creep happens.",null,"A second account doubles the attack surface and still leaves the old access in place.","Waiting for the next review leaves unnecessary payment access active for months."]],["ss21",0,2,"A system administrator is being involuntarily terminated. When should the administrator's access be disabled?",["At the end of the employee's last working day","At or just before the time the employee is notified","After the employee returns company equipment","Within 30 days, per the standard deprovisioning SLA"],1,"Disabling access at notification removes the chance for a disgruntled privileged user to cause damage.","Outline 2.3",["Waiting until the end of the day gives a privileged, possibly disgruntled administrator hours to cause harm.",null,"Equipment return is a separate step; access should not remain active while waiting for it.","A 30-day window is far too long for an involuntary termination of a privileged user."]],["ss22",0,2,"What is the PRIMARY purpose of periodic access recertification by managers?",["To force users to change their passwords","To measure how often users log in","To transfer account ownership to the security team","To detect and remove accumulated or inappropriate entitlements"],3,"Recertification confirms each user's access is still needed and catches privilege creep.","Outline 2.3",["Password changes are handled by password policy, not by managers recertifying who should have access.","Login frequency is an activity metric; recertification judges whether access is still appropriate.","Recertification keeps ownership with business managers; it does not hand accounts to the security team.",null]],["ss23",0,2,"In which access control model does the system enforce access using sensitivity labels and clearances, with users unable to change permissions?",["Mandatory access control","Discretionary access control","Role-based access control","Rule-based access control"],0,"MAC is enforced by the system based on labels; owners can't grant access at their discretion.","Outline 2.4",[null,"In DAC the owner decides who gets access, so users can change permissions on what they own.","RBAC grants permissions based on job roles, not on sensitivity labels and clearances.","Rule-based control applies global rules such as ACLs and does not rely on classification labels and clearances."]],["ss24",0,2,"A hospital with high staff turnover wants permissions to follow job functions such as nurse, physician and billing clerk. Which model is MOST appropriate?",["Discretionary access control","Mandatory access control","Role-based access control","Access control lists maintained per user"],2,"RBAC assigns permissions to roles, so users get the right access simply by being placed in a role.","Outline 2.4",["DAC lets individual owners grant access, which becomes inconsistent and hard to manage with high turnover.","MAC relies on labels and clearances, which is rigid and not designed around job functions such as nurse or clerk.",null,"Per-user ACLs must be edited for every hire and departure, the opposite of what high turnover requires."]],["ss25",0,2,"Access to a report must be allowed only for finance staff using a managed device during business hours. Which model BEST supports this policy?",["Role-based access control","Discretionary access control","Mandatory access control","Attribute-based access control"],3,"ABAC evaluates attributes of the user, device and environment (like time) in one policy.","Outline 2.4",["RBAC can check the finance role but not device status or time of day without extra attribute logic.","DAC relies on the owner granting access and cannot evaluate device or time conditions.","MAC compares labels and clearances and cannot express conditions like managed device and business hours.",null]],["ss26",0,3,"A server is valued at $200,000. A flood would cause 25% damage and is expected once every two years. What is the annualized loss expectancy?",["$50,000","$25,000","$100,000","$12,500"],1,"SLE = 200,000 x 0.25 = 50,000; ALE = SLE x ARO = 50,000 x 0.5 = 25,000.","Outline 3.1",["$50,000 is the single loss expectancy; it must still be multiplied by the ARO of 0.5.",null,"$100,000 would require an ARO of 2 applied to the SLE, but the flood is expected only once every two years.","$12,500 halves the correct result, as if the flood occurred only once every four years."]],["ss27",0,3,"An organization buys cyber insurance to cover losses from data breaches. Which risk treatment is this?",["Risk avoidance","Risk mitigation","Risk transfer","Risk acceptance"],2,"Insurance shifts the financial impact to a third party; it doesn't reduce the likelihood of the breach.","Outline 3.1",["Avoidance means stopping the risky activity altogether, such as not collecting the data.","Mitigation reduces likelihood or impact with controls; insurance does not make a breach less likely.",null,"Acceptance means taking the loss without action, while buying insurance actively shifts the financial impact."]],["ss28",0,3,"Who should formally accept the residual risk remaining after controls are applied to a business system?",["The business owner or senior manager with authority over the system","The security practitioner who performed the assessment","The external auditor","The system administrator"],0,"Risk acceptance is a management decision made by someone accountable for the business impact.","Outline 3.1",[null,"The practitioner assesses and recommends, but lacks the business authority to accept risk on the organization's behalf.","An external auditor must stay independent and only evaluates the controls; accepting risk would compromise that role.","The system administrator operates the system as a custodian and is not accountable for the business impact of the risk."]],["ss29",0,3,"Which BEST describes qualitative risk analysis?",["Rating likelihood and impact with categories such as high, medium and low","Calculating SLE, ARO and ALE in monetary terms","Using automated scanners to count vulnerabilities","Relying only on insurance actuarial tables"],0,"Qualitative analysis uses relative ratings and judgment rather than monetary values.","Outline 3.1",[null,"Calculating SLE, ARO and ALE in money is quantitative analysis, the opposite approach.","Counting vulnerabilities with scanners is an assessment activity and says nothing about rating likelihood and impact.","Actuarial tables supply numeric probabilities used in quantitative analysis, not relative high/medium/low ratings."]],["ss30",0,3,"Before sending logs that contain customers' personal data to a monitoring provider in another country, what should the analyst do FIRST?",["Encrypt the logs and send them","Remove the time stamps from the logs","Confirm the legal and privacy requirements for cross-border transfer with legal or compliance","Ask the provider to sign a non-disclosure agreement"],2,"Privacy and data residency laws may restrict the transfer, so obligations must be confirmed before acting.","Outline 3.2",["Encryption protects the data in transit but does not make an otherwise unlawful cross-border transfer legal.","Removing time stamps destroys the logs' usefulness and does nothing about the personal data they contain.",null,"An NDA is a contractual control, but legal transfer requirements must be confirmed before choosing contract terms."]],["ss31",0,3,"Which vulnerability scan gives the MOST accurate view of missing patches on servers?",["An unauthenticated external scan","An authenticated (credentialed) scan","A ping sweep","A passive network traffic scan"],1,"Credentialed scans can read installed software and configuration directly, reducing guesswork and false positives.","Outline 3.3",["An unauthenticated scan can only infer patch levels from banners and responses, producing more guesses and false positives.",null,"A ping sweep only discovers which hosts are up; it does not check installed software or patches.","Passive traffic analysis sees only what crosses the network and cannot inventory installed patches reliably."]],["ss32",0,3,"A scanner reports a critical vulnerability on a server, but the analyst confirms the relevant patch is installed and the service is not vulnerable. What is this finding?",["A false negative","A true positive","A zero-day","A false positive"],3,"The scanner reported a vulnerability that doesn't actually exist.","Outline 3.3",["A false negative is when a real vulnerability goes unreported; here a non-existent one was reported.","A true positive would mean the vulnerability really exists, but the analyst confirmed the server is patched.","A zero-day is a vulnerability with no patch available yet, while this finding concerns an already-patched issue.",null]],["ss33",0,3,"What must be obtained BEFORE a penetration test begins?",["A list of all employee passwords","Approval from the organization's internet service provider","Written authorization with an agreed scope and rules of engagement","A copy of last year's audit report"],2,"Without written authorization and scope, testing may be illegal and can disrupt operations.","Outline 3.3",["Testers must never collect all employee passwords in advance; that violates security policy and is not a prerequisite.","The ISP does not authorize testing of your systems; the system owner does, though you may notify providers as agreed.",null,"Last year's audit report may be useful background but it is not required, and it does not authorize testing."]],["ss34",0,3,"Which factor should MOST influence the order in which vulnerabilities are remediated?",["Severity combined with exploitability and the criticality of the affected asset","The order in which the scanner found them","The CVSS base score alone","How quickly each patch can be installed"],0,"Risk-based prioritization weighs severity against real exposure and business importance.","Outline 3.3",[null,"Scanner discovery order is arbitrary and reflects nothing about risk.","The CVSS base score ignores your environment, such as asset criticality and whether the flaw is exposed or actively exploited.","Ease of patching affects effort, but fixing easy low-risk issues first can leave critical exposures open."]],["ss35",0,3,"Correlating events from firewalls, servers and applications in a SIEM depends MOST on which of the following?",["Identical log formats on every device","Storing logs on the same disk as the SIEM","Using the same vendor for every device","Synchronized time across all log sources"],3,"Consistent time (NTP) lets events be placed in the correct sequence; the SIEM can normalize formats.","Outline 3.4",["SIEMs parse and normalize different log formats, so identical formats are not required.","Log storage location has no bearing on correlation, and co-locating logs with the SIEM does not order events.","A SIEM can ingest logs from many vendors; a single vendor is not needed for correlation.",null]],["ss36",0,3,"What is the BEST way to preserve log integrity if an attacker gains administrative control of a server?",["Increase the local log file size","Forward logs in real time to a centralized, access-restricted log server","Compress logs daily on the server","Set local logs to read-only for standard users"],1,"An attacker with admin rights can alter local logs; an independent central copy preserves the record.","Outline 3.4",["A bigger local log file stays on the compromised server where an administrator-level attacker can edit or delete it.",null,"Compressed logs remain on the server and can still be altered or wiped by an attacker with admin rights.","Read-only permissions for standard users do not stop an attacker who already has administrative control."]],["ss37",0,3,"To detect anomalous network activity, what must an analyst establish FIRST?",["A list of all known malware signatures","A baseline of normal activity","A penetration test report","A mandatory vacation policy"],1,"Anomalies can only be identified by comparison with what normal looks like.","Outline 3.5",["Malware signatures support signature-based detection, not anomaly detection, which needs a model of normal behaviour.",null,"A pen test report lists exploitable weaknesses but does not describe normal network behaviour.","Mandatory vacation is a personnel control for detecting fraud, unrelated to network anomaly detection."]],["ss38",0,4,"A responder confirms ransomware is encrypting files on a workstation. What should the responder do FIRST?",["Reimage the workstation from a known-good image","Run a full antivirus scan and let the user continue","Delete the ransom note and encrypted files","Isolate the workstation from the network"],3,"Containment stops the spread; reimaging first would also destroy evidence.","Outline 4.1",["Reimaging immediately destroys forensic evidence and does not stop the spread to other systems if the host is still networked.","Letting the user continue while scanning leaves the ransomware free to encrypt more files and shares.","Deleting the note and encrypted files destroys evidence and possibly recoverable data without stopping the malware.",null]],["ss39",0,4,"What is the PRIMARY purpose of a lessons-learned meeting after an incident?",["To improve processes and controls so similar incidents are prevented or handled better","To identify which employee should be disciplined","To close the incident ticket","To calculate the ransom that could have been paid"],0,"The post-incident review feeds improvements back into preparation.","Outline 4.1",[null,"Lessons-learned reviews should be blameless and focus on process improvements, not on picking someone to discipline.","Closing the ticket is an administrative step; the meeting's value is in the improvements it produces.","Calculating a ransom is not the goal; the review focuses on improving detection, response and prevention."]],["ss40",0,4,"Which of the following is BEST classified as an incident rather than an event?",["A single failed login by a user","A scheduled backup job completing","A confirmed unauthorized login to the payroll server","A firewall logging a blocked connection attempt"],2,"An incident is an event that actually harms or threatens confidentiality, integrity or availability.","Outline 4.1",["A single failed login is a normal event that happens routinely and does not by itself indicate harm.","A successful scheduled backup is a normal operational event, not an adverse one.",null,"A blocked connection shows the firewall working as intended; it is an event, not a confirmed compromise."]],["ss41",0,4,"Following the order of volatility, which should be collected FIRST from a running system?",["Hard disk image","Archived backup tapes","Logs already stored on a remote syslog server","Contents of system memory (RAM)"],3,"RAM is lost when the system is powered off, so it is collected before less volatile sources.","Outline 4.2",["A disk image is much less volatile than memory and survives a power-off, so it comes after RAM.","Archived backup tapes are among the least volatile sources and are collected last.","Logs already held on a remote syslog server are safe from local changes and are far less volatile than RAM.",null]],["ss42",0,4,"What is the MAIN purpose of chain of custody documentation?",["To speed up forensic analysis","To show evidence was controlled and unaltered from collection through presentation","To encrypt evidence for storage","To identify the attacker"],1,"Documented handling supports the integrity and admissibility of evidence.","Outline 4.2",["Chain of custody adds documentation steps; its purpose is proving integrity, not speeding up analysis.",null,"Encrypting evidence may protect it, but custody records exist to document who handled it and when.","Chain of custody tracks evidence handling; identifying the attacker is the goal of the analysis itself."]],["ss43",0,4,"Before analyzing a seized hard drive, what should the investigator do?",["Boot the drive in a workstation to review files","Run antivirus on the original drive to remove malware","Create a bit-for-bit image using a write blocker, verify it by hash, and analyze the copy","Defragment the drive to make analysis faster"],2,"Working on a verified image protects the original evidence from modification.","Outline 4.2",["Booting the original drive writes to it, altering timestamps and data and damaging evidentiary value.","Running antivirus on the original may delete or modify files, destroying evidence.",null,"Defragmenting rewrites the disk layout, destroys slack and unallocated data and alters the evidence."]],["ss44",0,4,"Which activity determines the maximum tolerable downtime of business processes?",["Business impact analysis","Vulnerability assessment","Penetration test","Tabletop exercise"],0,"The BIA identifies critical functions and the impact of their loss over time, which sets MTD, RTO and RPO.","Outline 4.3",[null,"A vulnerability assessment finds technical weaknesses, not how long business processes can be down.","A penetration test tests whether weaknesses can be exploited and says nothing about tolerable downtime.","A tabletop exercise tests a plan that already exists; it does not determine the MTD values the plan is built on."]],["ss45",0,4,"Management states that the order system can lose no more than four hours of transactions. Which metric does this define?",["Recovery point objective","Recovery time objective","Maximum tolerable downtime","Mean time between failures"],0,"RPO is the maximum acceptable data loss expressed as time.","Outline 4.3",[null,"RTO is how quickly a system must be restored, a measure of downtime rather than data loss.","MTD is the total time a process can be unavailable before the damage is unacceptable, not the acceptable data loss.","MTBF is a reliability measure of expected time between hardware failures, not a recovery objective."]],["ss46",0,4,"Operations must resume within a few hours of a disaster at an alternate location that already has equipment and near-current data. Which option is MOST appropriate?",["Warm site","Cold site","Hot site","Reciprocal agreement with a partner company"],2,"Only a hot site is ready to take over within hours.","Outline 4.3",["A warm site has some equipment but usually needs data restores and configuration, taking days rather than hours.","A cold site offers only space, power and connectivity, and can take weeks to become operational.",null,"Reciprocal agreements are hard to enforce and the partner's capacity may not be available within hours."]],["ss47",0,4,"In which disaster recovery test do team members talk through their roles in a scenario without activating any systems?",["Full interruption test","Tabletop exercise","Parallel test","Failover test"],1,"A tabletop is discussion-based; the others actually run recovery systems.","Outline 4.3",["A full interruption test actually shuts down primary operations and fails over, the most disruptive test.",null,"A parallel test brings up recovery systems alongside production to process real data.","A failover test actually switches workloads to the backup systems rather than only discussing roles."]],["ss48",0,4,"Which backup strategy requires only the last full backup and the most recent backup set to perform a restore?",["Full plus incremental","Incremental only","Snapshot of the operating system only","Full plus differential"],3,"A differential contains all changes since the last full backup, so only the latest one is needed.","Outline 4.3",["Full plus incremental needs the full backup and every incremental since it, not just the latest set.","Incremental-only has no baseline and would need the whole chain of backups to restore.","An operating system snapshot does not include user data and is not a full backup strategy.",null]],["ss49",0,5,"A policy requires that data on a stolen laptop be unreadable. Which control BEST meets this requirement?",["A strong login password","A cable lock","Full-disk encryption","Remote logging"],2,"Encryption at rest protects confidentiality even when the device is in someone else's hands.","Outline 5.1",["A login password can be bypassed by removing the drive and reading it in another machine.","A cable lock deters theft but does nothing once the laptop has been stolen.",null,"Remote logging records activity but does not make stored data unreadable to a thief."]],["ss50",0,5,"Which is the BEST way to verify that a downloaded installer has not been altered?",["Compare its SHA-256 hash with the value published on the vendor's trusted site","Check that the file size is the same as the vendor's","Scan it with antivirus","Confirm the file name matches the vendor's"],0,"A cryptographic hash changes if even one bit changes, so a match proves integrity.","Outline 5.1",[null,"Matching file size is weak evidence because an attacker can alter content while keeping the size the same.","Antivirus may catch known malware but cannot prove the file matches what the vendor published.","File names are trivially copied, so a matching name proves nothing about the content."]],["ss51",0,5,"Why do secure protocols use asymmetric cryptography to exchange a symmetric session key?",["Symmetric keys cannot be used for encryption over networks","Asymmetric crypto is faster for large amounts of data","It removes the need for certificates","Asymmetric crypto solves key distribution while symmetric crypto is faster for bulk data"],3,"The hybrid approach combines the strengths of both.","Outline 5.2",["Symmetric keys are routinely used for network encryption; the challenge is sharing them securely, not using them.","Asymmetric cryptography is much slower than symmetric, which is why it is not used for bulk data.","Hybrid key exchange still relies on certificates to authenticate the server's public key.",null]],["ss52",0,5,"To create a digital signature, the sender encrypts the message hash with which key?",["The sender's public key","The sender's private key","The recipient's public key","A shared symmetric key"],1,"Anyone can verify with the sender's public key, and only the sender could have produced it.","Outline 5.2",["The sender's public key is available to everyone, so using it would not prove who created the signature.",null,"Encrypting with the recipient's public key provides confidentiality for the recipient, not proof of the sender's identity.","A shared symmetric key can be used by both parties, so it cannot provide non-repudiation."]],["ss53",0,5,"What is the PRIMARY purpose of adding a unique salt before hashing passwords?",["To make the hash reversible for recovery","To defeat precomputed rainbow table attacks and hide identical passwords","To encrypt the password during transmission","To shorten the stored hash"],1,"A unique salt makes each hash different, so precomputed tables are useless.","Outline 5.2",["Salting does not make hashes reversible; hashing remains a one-way function.",null,"Salting happens at storage time and does not protect the password in transit; TLS does that.","A salt does not shorten the hash; the output length is fixed by the hash algorithm."]],["ss54",0,5,"An administrator must manage network devices remotely. Which protocol is the BEST replacement for Telnet?",["FTP","SNMPv1","HTTP","SSH"],3,"SSH encrypts the session, including credentials, whereas Telnet sends everything in clear text.","Outline 5.3",["FTP transfers files and, like Telnet, sends credentials in clear text.","SNMPv1 is for device monitoring and uses clear-text community strings, so it is not a secure remote shell.","HTTP is unencrypted web traffic and exposes credentials just like Telnet.",null]],["ss55",0,5,"Why are ECDHE cipher suites preferred for TLS?",["They provide forward secrecy, so past sessions stay protected if the server's private key is later compromised","They remove the need for server certificates","They use no public key cryptography","They make TLS compatible with SSL 2.0"],0,"Ephemeral key exchange means session keys can't be derived from the long-term key.","Outline 5.3",[null,"ECDHE suites still use server certificates to authenticate the server; ECDHE only handles key agreement.","ECDHE is elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman, which is itself public key cryptography.","SSL 2.0 is obsolete and insecure, and ECDHE suites are not about compatibility with it."]],["ss56",0,5,"Where should the private key of an organization's root certificate authority be stored for the strongest protection?",["On the CA server's system drive","In a password-protected file on a file share","In a hardware security module kept offline","In the certificate itself"],2,"HSMs generate and protect keys in tamper-resistant hardware, and an offline root reduces exposure.","Outline 5.4",["Storing the key on the system drive of a server exposes it to malware and anyone who compromises the server.","A password-protected file on a share can be copied and brute-forced offline.",null,"Certificates contain the public key; the private key is never embedded in the certificate."]],["ss57",0,5,"Which mechanism lets a client check the current revocation status of a single certificate in near real time?",["Certificate revocation list (CRL)","Certificate signing request (CSR)","Key escrow","Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP)"],3,"OCSP answers a query about one certificate; CRLs are published lists refreshed periodically.","Outline 5.4",["A CRL is a periodically published list of all revoked certificates, so it can be out of date and is not a per-certificate query.","A CSR is what an applicant sends a CA to request a certificate; it has nothing to do with revocation status.","Key escrow stores copies of keys with a trusted party for recovery and does not report revocation status.",null]],["ss58",0,6,"At which OSI layer do routers make forwarding decisions based on IP addresses?",["Layer 2 (data link)","Layer 3 (network)","Layer 4 (transport)","Layer 7 (application)"],1,"IP addressing and routing happen at the network layer.","Outline 6.1",["Layer 2 devices such as switches forward frames using MAC addresses, not IP addresses.",null,"Layer 4 handles end-to-end transport with TCP and UDP ports, not routing between networks.","Layer 7 is where applications like HTTP and DNS operate; routers do not forward based on it."]],["ss59",0,6,"An attacker sends forged ARP replies so traffic for the default gateway goes to the attacker's MAC address. Which countermeasure is MOST effective?",["Enable DNSSEC","Apply SYN cookies on servers","Enable dynamic ARP inspection on the switches","Change the Wi-Fi password"],2,"DAI validates ARP packets against trusted bindings and drops forged replies.","Outline 6.2",["DNSSEC protects DNS responses from forgery and does nothing against forged ARP replies on the LAN.","SYN cookies defend servers against SYN floods, not against ARP spoofing.",null,"Changing the Wi-Fi password does not stop an attacker already on the network from sending forged ARP replies."]],["ss60",0,6,"Which technique helps a server withstand a SYN flood without exhausting its connection table?",["SYN cookies","Port mirroring","MAC filtering","Increasing the DHCP lease time"],0,"SYN cookies avoid storing state for half-open connections until the handshake completes.","Outline 6.2",[null,"Port mirroring copies traffic to a monitoring port and does not reduce half-open connection state.","MAC filtering controls which devices can join a network and is easily bypassed; it does not help against spoofed SYN packets.","DHCP lease time affects address assignment and has nothing to do with TCP connection tables."]],["ss61",0,6,"Which control BEST protects clients from DNS cache poisoning?",["DNSSEC validation","Using DHCP reservations","Disabling ICMP","Using a shorter DNS time-to-live"],0,"DNSSEC signs DNS records so resolvers can verify origin and integrity.","Outline 6.2",[null,"DHCP reservations fix a client's IP address and do not verify the authenticity of DNS answers.","Disabling ICMP affects ping and error messages, not the integrity of DNS records.","A shorter TTL may shorten how long a poisoned record stays cached but does not prevent poisoning."]],["ss62",0,6,"Only devices with current patches and running antivirus should be allowed on the internal network. Which solution BEST meets this need?",["Static IP addressing","A web application firewall","Network access control with posture assessment","A load balancer"],2,"NAC checks device health before granting access and can quarantine non-compliant devices.","Outline 6.3",["Static addressing only fixes IP addresses and does nothing to check patch or antivirus status.","A WAF protects web applications from HTTP attacks and does not evaluate devices joining the network.",null,"A load balancer distributes traffic among servers and does not assess endpoint health."]],["ss63",0,6,"In an 802.1X deployment, what role does the network switch play?",["Supplicant","Authenticator","Authentication server","Certificate authority"],1,"The switch relays credentials between the supplicant and the RADIUS authentication server.","Outline 6.3",["The supplicant is the client device requesting access, not the switch.",null,"The authentication server, typically RADIUS, validates the credentials; the switch only relays them.","A certificate authority issues certificates that may be used in EAP-TLS but plays no part in relaying 802.1X traffic."]],["ss64",0,6,"Where should a public-facing web server be placed?",["On the internal user VLAN","On the same subnet as the database server","Directly on the internet with no firewall","In a screened subnet (DMZ) separated from the internal network"],3,"The DMZ lets outsiders reach the server while limiting their path to internal systems.","Outline 6.4",["Putting a public server on the user VLAN lets anyone who compromises it reach internal users directly.","Placing it beside the database means a compromised web server gives direct access to the most sensitive data.","An unfiltered internet placement exposes every service on the server to attack.",null]],["ss65",0,6,"Which IPsec protocol provides confidentiality for the packet payload?",["Authentication Header (AH)","Internet Key Exchange (IKE)","Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP)","Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE)"],2,"ESP encrypts the payload; AH only provides integrity and authentication.","Outline 6.4",["AH authenticates and protects integrity of packets but does not encrypt the payload.","IKE negotiates security associations and keys; it does not itself encrypt the data traffic.",null,"GRE is a tunneling protocol with no encryption of its own, often combined with IPsec for that reason."]],["ss66",0,6,"What should be the final rule in a firewall access control list?",["Deny all traffic not explicitly permitted","Allow all outbound traffic","Allow all traffic from internal networks","Log and allow all traffic"],0,"Implicit or explicit deny ensures anything not allowed by earlier rules is blocked.","Outline 6.5",[null,"Allowing all outbound traffic lets malware exfiltrate data and beacon out freely.","Trusting all internal traffic ignores insider threats and compromised internal hosts.","Logging and allowing everything would make the rest of the rule set pointless."]],["ss67",0,6,"Which device is placed inline and can automatically block malicious traffic it detects?",["Intrusion detection system on a span port","Network tap","Syslog server","Intrusion prevention system"],3,"An IPS sits in the traffic path and can drop packets; an IDS only alerts.","Outline 6.5",["An IDS on a span port receives only a copy of traffic, so it can alert but cannot drop packets inline.","A network tap passively copies traffic for monitoring and cannot block anything.","A syslog server collects log messages and has no ability to block network traffic.",null]],["ss68",0,6,"Which device is BEST suited to block SQL injection attempts against a web application?",["Packet-filtering router","Web application firewall","Network switch with port security","DHCP server"],1,"A WAF inspects HTTP requests at the application layer for attack patterns.","Outline 6.5",["A packet filter looks at addresses and ports only and cannot see SQL injection inside HTTP requests.",null,"Port security limits which MAC addresses can use a switch port and does not inspect application traffic.","A DHCP server assigns IP addresses and plays no part in filtering web requests."]],["ss69",0,6,"Which wireless configuration is MOST appropriate for a new enterprise deployment?",["WPA2-Personal with a shared passphrase","WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X authentication","WEP with a 128-bit key","Open network with MAC filtering"],1,"Enterprise mode gives each user individual credentials and WPA3 provides the strongest current protection.","Outline 6.6",["WPA2-Personal uses one shared passphrase for everyone, so there is no individual accountability and revoking one user is hard.",null,"WEP is broken and can be cracked in minutes regardless of key length.","An open network has no encryption, and MAC filtering is trivially bypassed by spoofing."]],["ss70",0,6,"Users connect to an access point broadcasting the corporate SSID that is actually run by an attacker. What is this attack?",["Bluejacking","War driving","Jamming","Evil twin"],3,"An evil twin impersonates a legitimate AP to intercept traffic or capture credentials.","Outline 6.6",["Bluejacking sends unsolicited messages over Bluetooth and does not involve a fake Wi-Fi access point.","War driving is searching for wireless networks from a vehicle, a reconnaissance activity rather than impersonating an AP.","Jamming disrupts wireless signals to deny service rather than luring users onto a fake network.",null]],["ss71",0,7,"Which type of malware spreads across networks on its own without user action?",["Worm","Trojan","Logic bomb","Macro virus"],0,"Worms self-replicate by exploiting vulnerabilities; the others need a trigger or user action.","Outline 7.1",[null,"A trojan disguises itself as legitimate software and relies on the user to run it; it does not self-propagate.","A logic bomb lies dormant until a trigger condition is met and does not spread across networks.","A macro virus needs a user to open an infected document to spread."]],["ss72",0,7,"Malware modifies the operating system kernel to hide its files and processes from administrators. What is it?",["Adware","Keylogger","Rootkit","Ransomware"],2,"Rootkits subvert the OS to conceal themselves and keep privileged access.","Outline 7.1",["Adware displays unwanted advertising and does not normally modify the kernel to hide itself.","A keylogger records keystrokes; hiding by modifying the kernel is characteristic of a rootkit.",null,"Ransomware announces itself by encrypting data and demanding payment rather than hiding in the kernel."]],["ss73",0,7,"Which observation is the STRONGEST indicator that an internal host is part of a botnet?",["A user reporting a slow browser","A failed Windows update","A full recycle bin","Regular outbound connections at fixed intervals to an unfamiliar domain"],3,"Periodic beaconing to unknown hosts is typical of command-and-control traffic.","Outline 7.1",["A slow browser has many harmless causes such as extensions or network congestion.","A failed update is common and usually benign, not a specific sign of botnet membership.","A full recycle bin is a normal housekeeping state with no security meaning.",null]],["ss74",0,7,"What is the MOST effective control to prevent unauthorized executables from running on public kiosks?",["Signature-based antivirus","Application allow-listing","A host-based intrusion detection system","User awareness training"],1,"Allow-listing blocks anything not explicitly approved, including unknown malware.","Outline 7.2",["Signature-based antivirus only blocks known malware and lets unknown or unsigned programs run.",null,"A HIDS detects and alerts on suspicious activity but does not by default prevent executables from running.","Training is of limited use on public kiosks used by anonymous members of the public."]],["ss75",0,7,"Which endpoint tool continuously records process activity and allows a responder to isolate a host remotely?",["Host-based firewall","Disk encryption software","Endpoint detection and response (EDR)","Patch management server"],2,"EDR provides behavioural telemetry, detection and response actions such as isolation.","Outline 7.2",["A host firewall filters network connections but does not record detailed process activity for investigation.","Disk encryption protects data at rest and provides no monitoring or response capability.",null,"A patch management server deploys updates and does not provide behavioural telemetry or host isolation."]],["ss76",0,7,"Before deploying a critical operating system patch to all production servers, what should the administrator do?",["Test the patch in a staging environment that mirrors production","Deploy it immediately to all servers at once","Wait until the vendor releases the next patch","Ask users whether they notice problems after installing"],0,"Testing catches compatibility problems before they cause outages, and supports a backout plan.","Outline 7.2",[null,"Deploying to all servers at once risks a widespread outage if the patch has compatibility problems.","Delaying a critical patch until the next release leaves known vulnerabilities open.","Relying on users to notice problems after production deployment is not testing and may cause outages first."]],["ss77",0,7,"A company phone containing sensitive email has been lost. Which MDM capability BEST protects the data?",["Remote wipe","Geofencing","Application whitelisting","Push notifications"],0,"Remote wipe removes the data from the lost device.","Outline 7.3",[null,"Geofencing triggers actions based on location but does not by itself remove data from a lost phone.","Application allow-listing controls which apps can run, which does not protect data already on a lost device.","Push notifications send messages to the device and provide no data protection."]],["ss78",0,7,"In a BYOD program, which MDM feature BEST separates corporate data from personal data?",["Full-device remote wipe","Carrier unlocking","Containerization","Jailbreak detection"],2,"A container keeps corporate apps and data apart so they can be managed and wiped without touching personal data.","Outline 7.3",["A full-device wipe erases personal data too, which is exactly what BYOD separation tries to avoid.","Carrier unlocking lets a phone work on other networks and has nothing to do with data separation.",null,"Jailbreak detection checks device integrity but does not separate corporate from personal data."]],["ss79",0,7,"Under the IaaS model, who is responsible for patching the guest operating system?",["The cloud provider","The cloud customer","The hypervisor vendor","The internet service provider"],1,"In IaaS the provider secures the physical and virtualization layers; the customer manages the OS and above.","Outline 7.4",["In IaaS the provider secures the physical hosts and virtualization layer, not the customer's guest operating system.",null,"The hypervisor vendor supplies virtualization software and has no access to or responsibility for guest OS patching.","The ISP provides connectivity only and has no role in patching cloud servers."]],["ss80",0,7,"A misconfigured cloud storage bucket exposed customer data. Which tool BEST detects such misconfigurations continuously?",["Network intrusion detection system","Data loss prevention on endpoints","Vulnerability scanner run once a year","Cloud security posture management (CSPM)"],3,"CSPM continuously checks cloud configurations against security policies and benchmarks.","Outline 7.4",["A network IDS watches traffic for attacks and does not evaluate cloud storage configuration settings.","Endpoint DLP monitors data leaving user devices, not the permissions set on cloud buckets.","An annual vulnerability scan is not continuous and typically does not check cloud service configurations.",null]],["ss81",0,7,"An attacker breaks out of a guest virtual machine to access the hypervisor. What is this called?",["VM sprawl","Hyperjacking by phishing","VM escape","Live migration"],2,"VM escape compromises the isolation between guests and the host.","Outline 7.5",["VM sprawl is the uncontrolled growth of unmanaged VMs, not an attack that breaks out of a guest.","Hyperjacking means taking control of the hypervisor, often by installing a rogue one; it is not delivered by phishing and does not describe breaking out of a guest.",null,"Live migration is a legitimate feature that moves a running VM between hosts."]],["ss82",0,7,"Unmanaged virtual machines are multiplying and many are unpatched. What is the BEST control?",["A formal provisioning process with inventory and lifecycle management for VMs","Increasing host memory","Taking more frequent snapshots","Moving all VMs to one host"],0,"VM sprawl is controlled by approval, tracking and decommissioning of virtual machines.","Outline 7.5",[null,"More host memory lets even more unmanaged VMs run and does nothing about patching or tracking.","Snapshots preserve VM state but add to sprawl and do not patch or inventory the machines.","Consolidating onto one host creates a single point of failure and does not bring the VMs under management."]]]);
